<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>However, it has been discovered through intense physical manipulation and scientific reasoning, that SCP-XXXX will respond to particular audio frequencies, all of which are outside the audible spectrum. It will then activate autonomously and begin drawing with extreme precision until its internal potential energy is exhausted.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To be clear: it activates if the crank is turned and not just if you like, ask it via particular audio frequencies? If so, what does 'will respond to' mean?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Though the Architect's whereabouts and physicality are yet unknown, there are many strange, otherworldly objects that lend themselves to prove his existence.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not really a fan of this angle. It feels too much like you're just throwing out a bunch of vague ideas you find kinda interesting in an attempt to make the position of the Architect more interesting; but it doesn't really and it doesn't really come across as interesting to me. More to the point, I'm not really sure how the Foundation figure out the Architect exists or like,,,what that actually means for this SCP or the world at large?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It has been noted that attempting to investigate and calculate these geometric patterns will result in Dissociative Amnesia, Psychosis, Dementia, Alzheimer's syndrome, and many other effects.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Generally I'm not a fan of things that just make you go crazy, particularly in a way that causes you to forget a lot; since like&#8230;it's super overplayed and often feels&#8230;kinda problematic and like it stigmatises mental illness more.</p>
<p>More to the point, it's also an effect that's really hard to convey in an interesting way within the confines of a SCP article.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is not yet known what happens when SCP-XXXX is allowed to finish one of its patterns, but it is certainly best left to conjecture.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>so like&#8230;what happens if it completes a pattern? How are you wanting to make this mystery interesting?</p>
<hr />
<p>My central concern with this article is that feels like it lacks cohesion: there's a lot of elements here (the architect, the madness, the patterns it draw being ominous in some way, the otherworldly objects the architect made) but I don't really see how they all come together to tell a compelling story. I am also concerned that the Foundation are barely mentioned here: a SCP article is told from their perspective, and they don't seem to really have any kind of role in this story, or any real effect on how it plays out, and a very passive point of view like this can be really hard to do well.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

