			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858062">
									

<blockquote>
<p>It is not yet known what happens when SCP-XXXX is allowed to finish one of its patterns, but it is certainly best left to conjecture.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Why? No need to be vague when trying to sell your idea</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is by no means something that could be elevator pitched, to say the least, and yes it will be incredibly difficult to achieve.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>And yet, this is what is required for greenlighting. I would suggest you streamline the idea and give us the overall points/plot and themes you're going for.</em></p>
<ul>
<li>Instead of listing out possible other things connected to this skip, focus on selling us why we would want to read about it. What are the stakes? What is the narrative? Even cosmic horror is dependent on an arc. Yes you can be vague and hinty in the article, but here you're meant to sell the idea.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>things that make you go crazy are a) boring, because it was done to death, and b) sorta demeaning to those who have atypical neuro states. But a) is the real problem for a story&#8230; I would suggest thinking of some other effect seeing the patterns does&#8230; The unbearable information driving someone crazy has been old news since the 1920s. Stretch your imagination, this automaton could make designs that could do anything&#8230; why make people crazy? What else could be done to make it unsettling?</li>
</ul>
<p>I suggest you take a good look at your ultimate point, and spell it out clearly in the idea post. You can edit it by clicking on options in the post and then edit.</p>

