			<div class="content" id="post-content-4858381">
									

<p>Thanks for the feedback.</p>
<p><strong>Spoiler below</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The reason why It's vague is that it is a mystery note that develops over time. I have to decide between balancing what to say here and what not to spoil in what is effectively the general outline of the story. The problem is that anyone who is actually interested in the concept enough to greenlight it would also end up being spoiled to the story in the long run. I explained in my spoiler post for Cyber exactly why it has the effects it has. It is definitely not just a simple &quot;it drives people insane&quot;, but the exact specifics of what it is doing and why it is doing it can fill out 400 words all on it's own and is a huge part of unraveling the mystery that I don't just want to spoil for readers.</p>
<p>In which care, should I care about that at all and just spill everything out?</p>
<p>My ultimate point is already written out as it pertains to the SPC's themselves.<br />
But spoilers kill a story, so I'm not really sure what to do here.<br />
And in my honest opinion, as it pertains to the mystery, I have already said too much in an effort to get greenlit for a sandbox that can't be justified in merely 400 words, but rather should be justified by the reading of the draft article itself.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>is the real problem for a story… I would suggest thinking of some other effect seeing the patterns does…</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not a problem at all, because its effects are not random in the slightest and it is not merely just &quot;driving someone insane&quot;. The idea here is that everything psychological that seems to be random is actually simply the extreme of things that already exist in the subjects, even in tiny quantities. The idea here is to put on a display that everyone, EVERYONE has some tiny piece of their mind that is not quite right, no matter how small. These patterns have hidden within them traps that latch on and exploit these small problems and cause them to grow to extremes, all in an effort to protect its creator's knowledge. Basically, the machine's patterns are not driving you insane or causing devastating mental diseases, they are simply magnifying the issues that are already there. The idea is that everyone, EVERYONE has some mental issue somewhere, no matter how small they may be. If that's not different enough of a tone then I don't really know what to say there. I'll take it back to the drawing board.</p>
<blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>why make people crazy? What else could be done to make it unsettling?</p>
</blockquote>
</blockquote>
<p>I noted that in the spoiler for Cyber, but that is really the question here. WHY is it doing what it is doing? And the answer is written, but hidden from view because that answer literally answers the mystery of the entire object. I'll probably rewrite or update the greenlight with some spoiler sections explaining the actual story at hand, so that people can read and avoid the context should they care to read along once the story is actually unfolding as an SCP, interviews and experiment logs.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

